## Novel Insights

The paper's most genuinely novel observation — that the effectiveness of guidance in diffusion planning correlates with the density of near-optimal trajectories in the training data (Figure 7b) — represents an actionable hypothesis that could reshape how practitioners select guidance strategies. Rather than treating CG/CFG as universally superior (the current default), this suggests a data-quality diagnostic that could guide method selection without additional training. The attention weight analysis (Figure 5b) revealing stride-invariant characteristic attention length is also a distinctive empirical finding: it suggests Transformer diffusion planners discover a planning abstraction that is naturally aligned to the task's temporal structure, independent of how many environment steps each planning step covers.

